{"causes":["The development of early Japanese writing systems","The introduction of Buddhism to Japan from Korea","The influence of Chinese culture on Japanese art and architecture"],"effects":["The creation of a distinct Japanese literary style","The spread of Shintoism throughout Japan","The development of a unique Japanese imperial system"],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Ō no Yasumaro","sourceName":"Ō no Yasumaro","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"Composed the Kojiki chronicle around 712 CE"},{"sourceSlug":"Heian Period","sourceName":"Heian Period","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"The Kojiki was written during the Heian period (794-1185 CE)"},{"sourceSlug":"Shintoism","sourceName":"Shintoism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"The Kojiki reflects the influence of Shintoism on Japanese culture and politics"},{"sourceSlug":"Chinese Culture","sourceName":"Chinese Culture","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"The Kojiki demonstrates the significant impact of Chinese culture on Japanese art, literature, and politics"},{"sourceSlug":"Taira Clan","sourceName":"Taira Clan","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"The Taira clan played a crucial role in the composition and transmission of the Kojiki"},{"sourceSlug":"Minamoto Clan","sourceName":"Minamoto Clan","verb":"PARTICIPATES_IN","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"The Minamoto clan also contributed to the composition and transmission of the Kojiki"},{"sourceSlug":"Japanese Imperial System","sourceName":"Japanese Imperial System","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"The Kojiki reflects the development of a unique Japanese imperial system"},{"sourceSlug":"Nihon Shoki","sourceName":"Nihon Shoki","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"The Kojiki contains many of the same entries as the Nihon Shoki"},{"sourceSlug":"Buddhist Monks","sourceName":"Buddhist Monks","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Kojiki","targetName":"Kojiki","context":"Buddhist monks played a significant role in the transmission and interpretation of the Kojiki"}],"places":[{"name":"Japan","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Nara","role":"Major battle"},{"name":"Heian-kyo","role":"Royal capital"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Kojiki","type":"Reference article","slug":"kojiki"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q813031","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kojiki"],"tags":["literary work","Histories & Chronicles","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"'Therefore, I, Yasumaro, with reverence and awe, have compiled and edited the old words, carefully selecting, and have respectfully presented them.' — Ō no Yasumaro, Preface to the Kojiki (712 CE)","legacySummary":"Kojiki contributed to the historical record of the Medieval period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Kojiki (古事記, 'Record of Ancient Matters') is the oldest extant chronicle of Japan, compiled in 712 CE under the imperial patronage of Empress Genmei (661–721) by Ō no Yasumaro (d. 723), who transcribed oral traditions recited by the imperial court memorist Hieda no Are. The Kojiki records the mythological origins of Japan — the creation of the Japanese islands by the gods Izanagi and Izanami; the birth of the sun goddess Amaterasu, the moon god Tsukuyomi, and the storm god Susanoo; Amaterasu's withdrawal into the Rock Cave of Heaven (Ama-no-Iwato) and subsequent emergence; the descent of Ninigi-no-Mikoto (Amaterasu's grandson) to the islands of Japan; and the founding of the imperial dynasty, traced through a line of legendary emperors to the historical Empress Suiko (r. 593–628). The text is written in classical Chinese with Japanese phonetic elements (man'yōgana).

The Kojiki is the primary mythological source of Shinto — Japan's indigenous religious tradition — and provides the narrative foundation for the veneration of kami (spirits/deities), the rituals of Shinto shrines, and the religious identity of the Japanese imperial family. Together with the Nihon Shoki (720 CE, compiled eight years later), it established the genealogy of the imperial family as direct descendants of the sun goddess Amaterasu, providing the theological legitimacy for the Japanese imperial institution — which continues in unbroken succession to the present day as the world's oldest reigning monarchy. The text's mythology — particularly the Amaterasu-Susanoo conflict, the Rock Cave episode, and Ninigi's descent — is fundamental to Japanese religious iconography, literature, theatre, and art across fourteen centuries.

The Kojiki exercised a pervasive and enduring influence on Japanese culture — its narratives were foundational for the noh drama, for classical Japanese poetry (the Man'yōshū draws on its themes), for the architectural tradition of Shinto shrines, and for the political ideology of the imperial state throughout Japanese history. The kokugaku (National Learning) scholars of the 18th century — particularly Motoori Norinaga, who produced the first complete scholarly commentary (Kojikiden, 44 volumes, 1798) — used the text to articulate a Japanese national identity independent of Chinese cultural influence. The text's mythology was mobilised in Meiji state ideology (1868–1912) and the ultra-nationalist period (1930s–1945) as the basis for the ideology of imperial divinity (kokutai) — formally renounced by Emperor Hirohito in his 1946 'Humanity Declaration.'","historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Kojiki is a vital cultural artifact that has shaped Japanese identity and informs national narratives.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
